// src/std/core/display.zen
//
// The display protocol. One method an impl defines — write yourself
// into a caller's buffer — and two sealed forms built on top of it,
// so a type can never print two different ways.
//
// The shape is deliberate: `toString` takes a Sink the CALLER owns,
// so a nested value writes into the buffer that is already open and
// nesting never allocates — and printing does not allocate at all,
// because `println` hands it the console it already holds. If it
// took a String instead, printing would need a String, a String
// needs an Alloc to grow, and `println` has no Alloc: printing
// would allocate behind the caller's back (law 1) or hello-world
// would need an arena. The allocating form is derived from the sink
// form rather than declared beside it, which is what makes "they can
// never diverge" a property of the code and not a promise in a
// comment.

Alloc = std.mem
String = std.text
Sink, WriteError = std.core.io

// every form here speaks WriteError, the union. a sink write fails
// with IoError, obtaining or growing a buffer fails with AllocError,
// and there is no From to paper over it, so the union is the honest
// type and .try() merges it into the caller's set for free.
//
// std.core.io owns both, and they are imported rather than reached
// through the prelude: this file is IN the prelude, so relying on it
// would make the surface depend on its own assembly order.

Display* = {
    // SEALED (=): the mechanical debug dump. Always there, for
    // every type, never overridden — which is what lets `{}` fall
    // back to it for a type that never defined a toString.
    //
    // STAGE 5. Its body is an @meta field-wise walk, and @meta is
    // outside the seed subset (PLAN.md 0.5), so the body arrives
    // with src/meta/meta.zen. DESIGN.md writes it, and it is kept
    // here verbatim so the gap is a body and not a design question:
    //
    //     out.add("{} {", @meta(self: @Self).name);
    //     @meta(self: @Self).fields.loop((h, field) {
    //         out.add(" {}: {},", field.name, field.value);
    //     });
    //     out.add(" }");
    //     Ok(());
    //
    // Until that body lands it cannot be written sealed, and of the
    // two bodiless forms only one is honest. REQUIRED (`= sig`) is
    // the OPPOSITE of sealed: it obliges every impl to supply the one
    // member they must never supply, and sema is right to say so. The
    // optional hook (`::= sig`) obliges nobody, which is what "always
    // there, never yours" degrades to while the body is missing.
    // At stage 5 this gains its body and becomes sealed.
    dump* ::= (self: @Self, out :: Sink) Res<(), WriteError>

    // OUTLINED (::=, no body): the pretty representation. Impls
    // define THIS one and nothing else, and the format machinery
    // routes `{}` through it, falling back to dump when a type has
    // none. It writes into a sink the CALLER owns, so a value nested
    // three deep still costs one buffer — and printing one costs no
    // buffer at all, because the sink is the console.
    toString* ::= (self: @Self, out :: Sink) Res<(), WriteError>

    // SEALED (=): the allocating form. It is DERIVED — it makes a
    // buffer and calls the outlined form — so an impl that defines
    // toString gets this one right for free, and no impl can make
    // the two disagree. Overload resolution picks between them on
    // the declared parameter type: a buffer or an allocator.
    toString* = (self: @Self, a: Alloc) Res<String, WriteError> {
        sb ::= a.String().try();
        self.toString(sb).try();
        Ok(sb);
    }
}
